### What is the Jump Game II problem?

The problem gives an array where each element represents the maximum jump length from that position. The task is to return the minimum number of jumps needed to reach the last index. It is guaranteed that the last index is reachable.

This can be modelled as a shortest-path problem where each index is a node and each index can reach the next `nums[i]` indices. BFS would give the minimum jumps, but we can optimise it to a greedy linear scan.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class JumpGameII {
    public int jump(int[] nums) {
        int jumps = 0;
        int end = 0;       // current window boundary
        int farthest = 0;  // farthest reachable from current window

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length - 1; i++) {
            farthest = Math.max(farthest, i + nums[i]);
            if (i == end) {
                jumps++;
                end = farthest;
            }
        }
        return jumps;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {2, 3, 1, 1, 4};
        System.out.println("Min jumps: " + new JumpGameII().jump(nums));
    }
}
```

```text
Min jumps: 2
```

### Why does greedy give the minimum jumps?

The greedy approach is equivalent to BFS where each jump is one BFS level. Within a level (window), we find the farthest position reachable. Since we always extend to the maximum farthest, each level covers the widest possible range, which means we use the fewest levels (jumps). This is the optimal substructure: extending to the farthest each time cannot be beaten.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class GreedyBFSEquivalence {
    // BFS level 0: index 0
    // BFS level 1: indices 1..nums[0] (one jump)
    // BFS level 2: indices (window end + 1)..farthest (two jumps)
    // ...
    // The greedy tracks these levels with just 3 variables.

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {2, 3, 1, 1, 4};
        System.out.println("Level 0: index 0");
        System.out.println("Level 1: indices 1..2 (jump 1)");
        System.out.println("Level 2: indices 3..4 (jump 2)");
        System.out.println("Reached end at level 2 => 2 jumps.");
    }
}
```

### How does the window boundary work?

The variable `end` marks the rightmost index reachable with the current number of jumps. As we scan from left to right, we update `farthest` at each index. When `i` reaches `end`, we have exhausted the current window and must take a new jump. At that point, we increment `jumps` and move `end` to `farthest`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class WindowBoundaryTrace {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {2, 3, 1, 1, 4};
        int jumps = 0, end = 0, farthest = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length - 1; i++) {
            farthest = Math.max(farthest, i + nums[i]);
            System.out.println("i=" + i + " nums[i]=" + nums[i]
                + " farthest=" + farthest + " end=" + end);
            if (i == end) {
                jumps++;
                end = farthest;
                System.out.println("  => Jump! jumps=" + jumps + " new end=" + end);
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Total jumps: " + jumps);
    }
}
```

```text
i=0 nums[i]=2 farthest=2 end=0
  => Jump! jumps=1 new end=2
i=1 nums[i]=3 farthest=4 end=2
i=2 nums[i]=1 farthest=4 end=2
  => Jump! jumps=2 new end=4
Total jumps: 2
```

### Why iterate only to n-2?

We iterate to `nums.length - 2` (the second-to-last index) because reaching or passing the last index inside the final window already counts the jump. If we iterated to the last index and `i == end` happened there, we would increment jumps one extra time, overcounting by one.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class WhyNMinus2 {
    // Example: nums = [1, 1, 1]
    // Correct: iterate i=0, i=1 (stop before i=2)
    //   i=0: farthest=1, i==end => jump=1, end=1
    //   i=1: farthest=2, i==end => jump=2, end=2
    //   Result: 2 jumps (correct)
    //
    // Wrong: iterate i=0, i=1, i=2
    //   i=2: farthest=3, i==end => jump=3 (WRONG, overcounted)

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {1, 1, 1};
        System.out.println("Correct (stop at n-2): 2 jumps");
        System.out.println("Wrong (include n-1): 3 jumps (overcounted)");
    }
}
```

### How is this different from Jump Game I?

| Feature | Jump Game I | Jump Game II |
|---|---|---|
| Question | can you reach the end? | what is the minimum jumps to reach the end? |
| Return type | boolean | integer |
| Approach | track farthest reachable | greedy window + jump counter |
| When to stop | farthest >= last index | iterate to n-2 |
| Complexity | O(n) time, O(1) space | O(n) time, O(1) space |

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class JumpGameIvsII {
    // Jump Game I: can you reach the end?
    public boolean canJump(int[] nums) {
        int farthest = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (i > farthest) return false;
            farthest = Math.max(farthest, i + nums[i]);
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {2, 3, 1, 1, 4};
        System.out.println("Can reach: " + new JumpGameIvsII().canJump(nums));
        System.out.println("Min jumps: " + new JumpGameII().jump(nums));
    }
}
```

```text
Can reach: true
Min jumps: 2
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include an array of length 1 (already at the end, 0 jumps), an array where the first element can reach the end directly (1 jump), and an array where every element is 1 (need n-1 jumps).

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class JumpGameEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        JumpGameII solver = new JumpGameII();

        System.out.println("Single element: " + solver.jump(new int[]{0}));        // 0
        System.out.println("Direct reach: " + solver.jump(new int[]{5, 1, 1, 1, 1})); // 1
        System.out.println("All ones: " + solver.jump(new int[]{1, 1, 1, 1}));     // 3
    }
}
```

```text
Single element: 0
Direct reach: 1
All ones: 3
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is iterating to the last index instead of n-2, which overcounts by one. Another mistake is not updating farthest before checking the window boundary, which misses the current index's contribution.

Wrong code:

```java
// WRONG: iterates to n-1, overcounts
for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) { // should be nums.length - 1
    farthest = Math.max(farthest, i + nums[i]);
    if (i == end) { jumps++; end = farthest; }
}
```

Correct code:

```java
// CORRECT: stops at n-2
for (int i = 0; i < nums.length - 1; i++) {
    farthest = Math.max(farthest, i + nums[i]);
    if (i == end) { jumps++; end = farthest; }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer connects this to BFS layer counting and recognises it as the greedy compression of a BFS. They know that the DP approach with `dp[i] = min jumps to reach i` is O(n squared) and the greedy is O(n) because it avoids recomputing ranges. At National Bank of India, the same window-expansion logic applies to routing settlement messages through relay stages with minimum hops.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class SeniorJumpNotes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Senior insights:");
        System.out.println("  1. Greedy is BFS compressed to 3 variables.");
        System.out.println("  2. DP approach is O(n^2), greedy is O(n).");
        System.out.println("  3. Iterate to n-2 to avoid overcounting.");
        System.out.println("  4. Same pattern: minimum relay hops in settlement.");
    }
}
```
